intro = '<section class="bookpanel" id="guide"><div class="chapterhead"><span class="eyebrow">FIELD GUIDE / START HERE</span><h2>Understand it. Then run it.</h2><p>A practical command book for learning Kali, maintaining your VM and exploring an authorized security lab.</p></div><div class="callout"><strong>Your command line is inside Kali.</strong><p>Open Terminal in your Kali desktop. A downloaded Windows folder is not automatically a directory inside the Linux guest. Follow the context label: terminal examples run in Kali, Shodan queries in its website, and SDK snippets in Python.</p></div><h3>Anatomy of a command</h3><div class="syntax"><span>nmap<small>PROGRAM</small></span><span>-sT<small>SCAN OPTION</small></span><span>-p 8000<small>OPTION + VALUE</small></span><span>127.0.0.1<small>TARGET: THIS KALI VM</small></span></div><div class="flow" aria-label="Learning workflow"><span><b>01 / Observe</b><small>Read help &amp; scope</small></span><i>→</i><span><b>02 / Run</b><small>Use a small lab</small></span><i>→</i><span><b>03 / Interpret</b><small>Check the result</small></span><i>→</i><span><b>04 / Record</b><small>Save your findings</small></span></div><div class="guidegrid"><article class="panel"><h3>Read the examples</h3><p>Command blocks are copyable. Do not add a $ or # prompt. Paths and filenames are case-sensitive. Quotes keep paths with spaces together.</p><p>Examples assume you created <code>$HOME/kali-lab</code> in Lab 04. Some tools and input files need installing or creating first.</p><button class="btn primary" type="button" data-view="labs">Start Guided Lab 04 →</button></article><article class="panel"><h3>Know the effect</h3><p>Each entry labels whether it reads information, writes files, changes settings or sends traffic. Expand its explanation to read flags and expected results.</p><p>Use network tools only on your systems or an explicitly authorized lab. Output is evidence to interpret, not proof that a system is secure.</p><button class="btn" type="button" data-view="comphardware">Read computer foundations →</button></article><article class="panel"><h3>Use the full catalog</h3><p>Kali contains many programs, not one command language. This book covers 499 lessons and examples plus 45 expanded concept guides; it is not every tool or flag.</p><p><code>man TOOL</code>, installed help and the official Kali catalog provide complete, version-specific details. Not every tool is installed by default.</p><button class="btn" type="button" data-view="sources">Open official references →</button></article><article class="panel"><h3>Keep your lab recoverable</h3><p>Back up important files before exercises that change them. Snapshots help restore a VM but consume storage. Find the virtual disk location in your VM settings.</p><p>Start with localhost. The address <code>192.0.2.10</code> is a placeholder lab VM, not a discovered target. A subnet example must match your approved lab network.</p></article></div></section>'
